Subject: [PATCH] linux-atm: fix the remaining 34 pointer-signedness warnings by correcting declarations

No -Wno-* switch and no blanket cast: in each cluster the wrong side was
identified from the surrounding convention, then the declaration was fixed, and
the cascade that followed was followed rather than papered over.

mpoad - packets are assembled through uint8_t *. packets.h declares the frame
fields uint8_t, p_factory.c works on `uint8_t buffer[MAX_PACKET_LENGTH]` /
`uint8_t *reply` and writes fields with `*((uint32_t *)(buff + pos))`, and
mpc_control's LEC_ADDRESS / MPS_MAC_ADDRESS / OWN_ATM_ADDRESS / elan_name are all
`unsigned char` arrays (packets.h:14/15/19/20). io.h's `char *buff` packet
parameters are therefore the odd ones out. send_to_mps, send_to_dataplane,
create_ingress_svc and the static create_shortcut become uint8_t *, their
definitions and internal assembly cursors (`char tmp[]`, `char *pos = tmp`)
follow, and the two receive buffers feeding recognize_packet(uint8_t *) become
uint8_t *. lecs.c's request/reply path lines up the same way; elan_name is text
stored in a uint8_t[32] field, so strcpy/strlen there get an explicit (char *)
on both operands rather than a silent sign change.

led - the address APIs already say unsigned char * (address.h:29 addr_getesi,
display.h:8 mac2text, join.h:6 init_lec_params, kernel.h:31 kernel_init) and
bus_mac / well_known_lecs are already unsigned char. MAC and ESI values are
octet strings, so main.c's `char mac_addr[ETH_ALEN]` and `char esibuff[20]` and
join.c's four `char buff[MAX_CTRL_FRAME]` LANE control-frame buffers were the
wrong side. The `(struct ctrl_frame *)buff` casts and the tlvp cursor stay
consistent.

ilmid - atmdev.h:166 declares `signed char vpi_bits; signed char vci_bits;`, so
put_ci's `char *bits` was simply the wrong type for `&ci.vpi_bits`. Changed to
match the header instead of casting the callers.

include/atmd.h - UN_CTX.size is passed straight to recvfrom() as its
socklen_t *addrlen out-parameter and to sendto() as socklen_t. It was `int`.
Patch 700 already fixed the same int-vs-socklen_t confusion in arpd/io.c's
get_local(); this is the last instance.

maint/saaldump.c - the captured PDU buffer is raw AAL5 bytes handed to
analyze(unsigned char *pdu, ...); `char buf[ATM_MAX_AAL5_PDU]` was the anomaly.
(The other `char buf[MAX_ITEM]` at line 48 is a text line buffer that inspects
buf[len-1] == '\n' and is left as plain char.)

ilmid/mib.c - IpAddress.octs is AsnOcts' `char* octs`, which cannot be widened
to unsigned or const without breaking every write-through and free() in the asn1
library (see 706). The cast stays at that one assignment, and the local `p`
deliberately keeps its unsigned type: the very next statement prints p[0..3] with
%d as an IPv4 address, and a plain char would render octets above 127 negative.
